  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 1200 AM CST Sat Nov 28 2026\n\nBroad zonal flow aloft over the CONUS will keep the upper low moving \nprogressively east Sunday night while another disturbance begins \nentering the Pacific NW. This spacing between systems will give the \nsurface ridge time to settle early which should lead to more light \nfreezes for Monday morning, mainly over the Hill Country. The return \nto the warming trend will be faster this time, and the return flow \nwinds will have all areas looking at a southerly breeze by the end \nof the day. The warming southerlies continue through Wednesday, and \nby that time, the south winds will be deep enough to pull plenty of \nmoisture onshore and bring a mostly cloudy sky for Wednesday. \n\nFinally the next polar upper low arrives for Thursday, and this one \nhas more southerly dynamics to impact TX more directly than previous \nsystem. PoPs in the NBM are trending in the right direction with \nnorthern and eastern counties getting some mid to high chances (45-\n65% for daytime Thursday. While the dynamics are better for \ngenerating convection, the trailing ridge to follow would suggest a \nweaker cold air advection pattern with seasonably mild temperatures \nfor late in the week.",
